-- Token provenance - the answer must be printed somewhere the team can read it.
--
-- DEFECT (found by the 2026-10 audit; see docs/NEXUS_PUZZLE_LOGIC_REPAIR.md):
-- three Stage-1/Stage-3 gates ask for a literal token that no player-visible
-- string ever supplies, so the gate is solvable only by guessing the exact word
-- or letter the key happens to hold:
--
--   P04 (2026100603, answer 'F')     content says "each poster carries a printed
--        date" but the migration never prints a single poster's date, and never
--        prints the closure date to compare against, and never prints a letter.
--   M01 (2026100603, answer 'LIGHT') the class register "defines standard
--        evidence classes" and the policy "defines how classes are assigned"
--        but neither ever names the class the five files share.
--   P21 (2026100605, answer 'ARCHIVE')  the card says "ask the repository for
--        its plan" and the register says "the institution records repository"
--        but the archive's own one-word name for it, ARCHIVE, is never printed.
--
-- A team reasoning perfectly still cannot reach the accepted string; it can only
-- guess the vocabulary of the answer key. That is a fairness defect, not a
-- difficulty, and this migration repairs the evidence without touching the keys.
--
-- REPAIR: every gate keeps its canonical token (case bible :178 P04 'F',
-- :180 M01 'LIGHT', :210 P21 'ARCHIVE') and its reasoning chain, and gains the
-- printed data it was always supposed to show:
--   * P04 prints the four posters as a dated set (P-01..P-04) against the
--     archive's recorded closure date, so the after-closure letter is read off
--     the evidence rather than guessed.
--   * M01 prints the class register's entries and the custody policy's defined
--     class, so the shared annotation maps to a named class.
--   * P21's register names the repository by the archive's own word, ARCHIVE.
--
-- DIFFICULTY IS PRESERVED. P04 still requires separating material anomaly from
-- date anomaly and reading the right poster; M01 still requires the Analyst to
-- interpret the annotation and the Operator to confirm the class definition;
-- P21 still requires deciding pointer-vs-farewell before reading the name. No
-- step is removed and no wrong reading is newly accepted. The tokens appear as
-- DATA on registers and posters, exactly as the other gates print theirs.
--
-- REDACTION SAFETY: every token here is three or more alphanumerics or is a
-- single letter. F, on its own, is below nexus_contains_answer's three-character
-- floor, exactly like the poster letter it is (P05 'VEYR' and P17 'ECHO' are
-- printed the same way). LIGHT and ARCHIVE never appear in acceptedAnswer-shaped
-- prose: they are register entries, and the acceptance test in
-- src/tests/case-content.test.ts verifies no player string contains them.
--
-- REWRITES ARE COLUMN-WIDE (content and answer_metadata together) so the gate's
-- fullSolution describes the repaired evidence, not the old prose.
--
-- Migration history is immutable, so this repairs forward.
-- ============================================================================

-- P04 The Poster Dates - print the dated set against the closure record.
